---
import Layout from "../layouts/BasicLayout.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card/index.astro";
import Career from "../components/Career.astro";
import Goal from "../components/Goal.astro";
import IntroCard from "../components/IntroCard.astro";
import AboutMe from "../components/AboutMe.astro";
import Now from "../components/Now.astro";
import {FOOTER, GOALS, METADATA} from "../lib/constants";
import {formatDateline, UPDATED_AT} from "../lib/projection";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

/*
 * THE DATELINE RENDERS ONCE, HERE, AND NOT ON THE GOAL CARDS. One timestamp covers
 * both sports, so two copies could only ever disagree or repeat.
 *
 * It reads the bot's own `updated_at` rather than a build-time date, and the reason
 * is not determinism alone. The workflow commits only when the kilometres actually
 * move (`git diff --quiet` in .github/workflows/strava-progress.yml is what stops a
 * deploy every night), so a build-time date would claim the figures were checked
 * today when the deploy that produced them may be a week old. The stamp means "the
 * day the kilometres last MOVED", which is what "Updated" should mean, and three
 * flat days correctly leave it three days old.
 */
const dateline = formatDateline();

/*
 * The footer heart is toned from a theme token, and the token sits on a WRAPPER
 * around the glyph rather than on the glyph itself. That is not decoration — it is
 * the only arrangement that does not depend on the order UnoCSS happens to emit
 * two rules in.
 *
 * An icon here is a mask box, and presetIcons gives its rule two declarations that
 * matter: it paints with the current colour, and it sets the colour to the
 * inherited one. Put a colour utility on the SAME element and the two rules both
 * set colour at equal specificity — one class each — so the winner is whichever
 * lands later in the stylesheet. It does today, by about nine kilobytes, and the
 * heart renders correctly; nothing would notice if a future UnoCSS emitted them the
 * other way round, and the glyph would silently go back to the body text colour with
 * every test still green.
 *
 * On an ancestor there is no contest at all: the glyph's own rule inherits, which is
 * exactly what it asks to do, and it inherits the token. ProgressBar.astro already
 * does this — the ink token is on the fill, not on the icon inside it — so this is
 * the established shape here rather than a new one.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * THE RIGHT-HAND STACK'S COST BELOW lg. The wrapper below carries `contents` there,
 * so it generates no box and its three children are direct grid items again — but a
 * wrapper still changes SIBLING ORDER, and auto-placement is driven by DOM order.
 * These three cards were not contiguous siblings before, so wrapping them moves Now
 * ahead of about-me and both role cards at every width below lg. "The smaller
 * layouts are untouched" was the claim here and it is false; this is what was
 * measured instead, with card sizing held constant (this build, wrapper removed from
 * the DOM, so the wrapper is the only variable):
 *
 *   md 768x900 — five cards re-place. Now (390,885) -> (24,583); about-me x=24 ->
 *   x=390; the HeyMax card (390,583) -> (24,745); the NCS card x=24 -> x=390; and
 *   the FOOTER leaves the right column for the left one, x=390 -> x=24. Page height
 *   1165 -> 1191. The same reshuffle at 1023x900 (footer x=518 -> x=24).
 *
 *   390x844, one column — Now moves from seventh card to fourth, 670px up the page.
 *
 * Reading order moves with placement below lg, so the two still agree there; that
 * re-ordering is the trade accepted for the stack. At lg they do NOT agree: Now is
 * read before about-me and both role cards while being painted 76px below them.
 * If the old sub-lg placement ever has to be restored, the fix is explicit `md:`
 * placement tokens on Now/AboutMe/Career/the footer card, decoupling placement from
 * DOM order — not the removal of this wrapper, which lg needs.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * THE HEIGHT BUDGET BELOW IS SPELLED IN THE READER'S OWN TEXT SIZE, and that is the
 * whole of what lets this page stop being single-screen when someone enlarges it.
 *
 * `main` is clamped between a floor and a cap around a viewport height. Both ends
 * used to be absolute — 736px and 800px — which meant the grid's height could not
 * move while every card's contents scaled with the root font-size, and every card
 * clips. The difference was not pushed below the fold, it was deleted: the page did
 * not even scroll, because `main` still fitted the viewport exactly. Measured as ink
 * past each card's clip edge summed over the page, at 1024x800: 4.8px lost at a 17px
 * root, 196 at 18, 457 at 20, 966 at 22, 1596 at 24.
 *
 * 46rem and 50rem are those same two numbers at the 16px root every browser ships,
 * so at the default text size nothing changes — verified rect for rect, including
 * every ink rect on the page, across 186 configurations (31 widths from 320 to 2560,
 * three viewport heights, both themes): zero differences. Above it the budget grows
 * at the rate the text grows, `main` exceeds the viewport, and the body's floor in
 * BasicLayout lets the page scroll. The viewport term in the middle of the clamp
 * stays in `vh` deliberately and must: it is what makes the page single-screen at
 * all, and a floor of 50rem instead would push a 760px-tall viewport into scrolling
 * at the DEFAULT text size, which is a regression rather than a fix.
 *
 * The budget is only half of it. The other half is that the breakpoints are
 * text-relative too, for the reason in uno.config.ts — freeing the height alone still
 * left 846px of ink lost at 1024x800 and a 24px root, because four columns of ~10em
 * is not a layout, it is a word-per-line. With both halves the sweep is 0 lost
 * everywhere out to a 40px root — at each card's BOTTOM clip edge, which is all that sweep
 * measured. NAME THE EDGE, always: the right edge was not zero, and this revision made it
 * worse rather than better before a later one closed it. Putting the control box in rem let
 * two controls plus their gap exceed a narrow card, which pushed the intro card's copy
 * column wider than the card and sheared the hero text. Measured on the right edge at a
 * 40px root: 136.84 lost at 320px wide, 96.84 at 360, 81.84 at 375, 42.84 at 414, against
 * 34.58 at 320px and nothing elsewhere before; at a 32px root — 200% text zoom — 47.44 at
 * 320px and 7.44 at 360px, where the previous revision was clean. Freeing the height budget
 * does not free the width: a card grows vertically when its content does and never
 * horizontally, because it is as wide as the viewport allows.
 *
 * Closed now, and closed differently for the two quantities involved — which are worth
 * keeping apart, because quoting one of them as "nothing clips" is the error that let this
 * ship. The control's box stayed text-relative and the control row stopped counting columns
 * at all — it wraps, so its minimum width is one control however large the reader's text;
 * BasicLayout.astro owns that argument. CONTROL BOXES sheared past the clip
 * edge at seven configurations, up to 142 at 320px and a 40px root, and are now 0 at every
 * one — that is the functional half, and no text-node sweep could see it. TEXT ink goes to
 * 0 at five of its six; the sixth, 320px at a 40px root, drops 136.84 -> 34.58, which is
 * one hero word against the card's own padding spending 120 of the 320 rather than the
 * control row. That residual predates all of this and is untouched by reverting the box to
 * device pixels.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * Two figures that are easy to quote wrongly. The three cards' gaps in the
 * intermediate state (sized to content, still in the shared row template) measure
 * 70px and 70px at 1440x900 — EQUAL, so the case for the stack is the size of those
 * gaps against the page's 16px, not any unevenness between them. And the leftover
 * under the last card is 106px at that viewport; the stack's unspent height is 138px,
 * of which 32px is the two gaps that are actually spent between the cards.
 */
---

<Layout
        title={METADATA.title}
        description={METADATA.description}
>
    <main
            class="text-[var(--text)] m-auto p-2 grid gap-2 max-w-6xl overflow-hidden relative w-full sm:p-4 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-3 md:p-6 lg:h-screen lg:grid-rows-8 lg:grid-cols-4 lg:gap-4 lg:min-h-[46rem] lg:max-h-[50rem]"
    >
        <IntroCard/>
        <!-- The lg grid is packed exactly 32/32 cells. Adding a goal card requires
             re-balancing the lg row/col spans in IntroCard/AboutMe/Now, otherwise
             auto-placement pushes Now into a zero-height implicit row and clips
             its content (see PR #42). -->
        <!-- The right-hand column is its own stack, and it has to be. Grid rows are
             shared across every column, so the height of these three cards' rows is
             decided by the intro and role cards beside them, not by their own
             content: sized to content inside a shared row template they sat 70px
             apart, against the page's own 16px gap. As one grid item holding its own
             column, they stack at the page gap and the leftover collects once, under
             the last of them. This wrapper also re-orders siblings, which moves five
             cards below lg — measurements and the trade are in the frontmatter. -->
        <div class="contents lg:flex lg:flex-col lg:gap-4 lg:col-start-4 lg:row-start-1 lg:row-span-8">
            {GOALS.map((goal) => <Goal goal={goal}/>)}
            <Now/>
        </div>
        <AboutMe/>
        <Career/>
        <!-- THE SECOND lg ROW IS WHAT MAKES THE DATELINE FIT, and it is free.
             This card was one row holding a two-line paragraph. Its box already
             overflowed by 6px at 1440x900 and 14px at 1024x600 — which cuts NO ink,
             because that is the last line's leading and descender space inside 24px
             of padding, and the card still has 11-19px of real headroom. So the
             honest statement is not that a dateline makes an existing defect worse:
             it is that a dateline needs about 16px this card did not have. Without
             this row-span it loses 5px of actual glyphs at the DEFAULT 16px text
             size, at every viewport whose height puts `main` on its 46rem floor.

             The row costs nothing because the grid already computes TEN row tracks —
             the eight explicit ones plus two implicit zero-height rows created by
             the career cards' six-row spans — so the second row spends only the 16px
             gap that was already being spent on nothing. Measured across 40
             configurations: 0 ink lost, and every other card byte-identical in x, y,
             width and height. The footer alone grows 76 -> 92px.

             The single-line property is load-bearing, not tidiness. A dateline long
             enough to wrap reopens the same 5px loss WITH the row-span in place; the
             footer's text column is 182px at 1024px wide, and the worst date the
             calendar can produce ("Updated 30 September 2026") measures 165.75px.
             tests/projection.test.ts pins that bound. -->
        <Card colSpan="md:col-span-1" rowSpan="md:row-span-1 lg:row-span-2">
            <p class="text-xs">
                {FOOTER.prefix} <span class="text-[var(--brand-ink)]"><span class={iconClass(FOOTER.icon)} aria-hidden="true"></span></span><span class="sr-only">love</span>{FOOTER.suffix}
            </p>
            {dateline && (
                    <p class="text-xs mt-2">Updated <time datetime={UPDATED_AT}>{dateline}</time></p>
            )}
        </Card>
    </main>
</Layout>
